The two sentence-opener rules, a sentence that starts with "So" and one that starts with "There is" or "There are", follow write-good: https://github.com/btford/write-good, commit 6940b034c6f5a7e101c01a24d651a778fc3fe435.
MIT, in write-good/LICENSE.
Copyright (c) 2014-2019 Brian Ford

The weasel words are from weasel-words, the package write-good takes them from: https://github.com/btford/weasel-words, commit 642f5981e3fdfbe82ca2b100474aade3fd9eb943, the first with a licence file (added in 6ab75282e5b115f812f1d1bc1997633aa700a4f5). All 27 words at that commit are taken. That is one more than the newest release on npm, 0.1.1, which write-good installs: "usually" came in after it. Like weasel-words, furbish doesn't flag "many" or "few" straight after "too": the rows "too many" and "too few" have the kind none, which flags nothing and keeps the word inside it from being flagged. A longer phrase from another list that starts inside one, such as "few in number", is still flagged. Unlike weasel-words, this also holds for "Too" at the start of a sentence and across a line break.
MIT, in write-good/LICENSE-weasel-words.
Copyright (c) 2014 Brian Ford


diction/words.tsv

From GNU diction 1.11, https://www.gnu.org/software/diction/, the release tarball diction-1.11.tar.gz (sha256 35c2f1bf8ddf0d5fa9f737ffc8e55230736e5d850ff40b57fdf5ef1d7aa024f6).
GPL-3.0-or-later: its source files and README grant version 3 "or (at your option) any later version". The licence text is its COPYING file, here as diction/LICENSE. The English phrase file, en, has no header of its own and comes under the package's licence.
Copyright 1997-2007 Michael Haardt

Taken from the file en: the 26 phrases of proselint's cliches/diction list that it holds, all marked there as clichés to avoid, word for word, with "you are hereby advised" as en has it (proselint adds "that"). "we are pleased to advice" is spelled as in GNU diction, so it only flags that misspelling.


